what is going on everybody and welcome  to my initial thoughts on the Amazon  echo dot version two so for me this is  actually my first echo device ever I  didn't buy the original one just because  I thought they were a little expensive  for what I needed I just I simply didn't  need another nice speaker so the dot two  the first thing about it is its size  it's it's pretty small and it can pretty  much be stuffed wherever you want in  your house and probably the best thing  about this device is the microphone on  it at least in my house I've got 1,800  square-foot house my office here is in  the back corner of my house and I can go  to the opposite side opposite corner of  my house and still engage with the  device without having to yell I've got  to raise my voice but I have to yell so  that's actually pretty impressive so  that was the first thing the second  thing is it does have a speaker on it  and it's actually on the bottom here and  surprisingly it works pretty well it  they like the speaker part kind of like  lifts up off of the ground just enough  to have the sound waves like bounce off  and you think that might sound kind of  weird but it actually doesn't so that's  that now for the price it was all it was  like $50 so when the echos first kind of  came out there was some hacks for how to  use like a Raspberry Pi to make your own  echo device and those were nice but at  50 dollars you're really not going to be  able to build your own echo device for  cheaper like this is as good as it's  gonna get so that's pretty impressive so  now some kind of downsides that I found  was one so with echo the skills are kind  of what makes the device so it comes  with some skills you can ask it to play  music and you can do a bunch of Amazon  things so if you're going to tie it to  your Amazon account like your Prime  account for example you can do that  really easily but the downside is if you  if you're trying to do other things  besides that if you go into the skills  like you would like maybe within your  app store with a phone you would expect  to find all kinds of really neat apps  well in the skill store that's just  really not the case now there could be a  whole lot of reasons for this I think  the main thing though is the skills  there's really no easy way to mana  heis the skills so with like phone apps  you can put ads on them or you can  charge like a dollar for the app and  that just that just works it makes it  easy for even small developers to kind  of enter into the market whereas with  the echo in the skills section of the  app there is no way to charge for a  skill so all the skills that you're  seeing there are just really basic where  there's really nothing there so they're  like facts or jokes or some news but  very light even on the news so that's  kind of a bummer so especially either  for a user or developer it's kind of  hard to get in there obviously you could  do things like have like audio ads or  you can have in-app purchases of some  kind also I've seen some people will  actually you know the echo is maybe  better thought of as a remote control  and that's it it doesn't do anything  else so then you tie that remote control  to something else some sort of web  service or even a phone app so you could  have it tied to some sort of mobile app  that you do charge for so there are  options but from what I'm seeing in the  skill store it's just not very popular  so the one thing that I really like and  I'll just demo it really quick is that  when you speak to the device you don't  have to pause after you say the devices  name which is one of the most annoying  things for me on the phone I think they  do it on the phone because the  microphone just can't always be on so it  like turns on then activates whereas  with Alexa  I don't really know maybe the microphone  really is just always on and listening  but on your like let's say on an Android  or even an Apple device so with Android  you'll say you know okay Google and then  you'll you'll have to like yes a wait a  second for like okay Google is start up  and go but that's okay I can understand  okay the microphones off but the thing  that really irritates me is like for  example when you're setting an event  with Google you'll set the event you'll  give it a title and then Google says is  this right do you want to save and but  if you answer it normally like if it  says do you want to save and you say  immediately yes so it's like do you want  to save yes if you do that it doesn't  work it doesn't register your yes right  and that's really annoying you have to  like wait and then say yes that's  annoying it's very clunky it's not  natural language so just  quickly let me pull this down here Alexa  play music shuffling your music Alexa  stop okay so it's it's really fast at UM  at the commands you don't have to have  this kind of arbitrary useless pause  when you you engage with it so anyway  those are just my initial thoughts I  think for fifty dollars you really can't  go wrong as long as you have something  you actually want to use it for so  probably one of the most popular things  is like the the Philips hue light bulbs  so you can get those lights and then  command the lights with your Alexa and  so that's pretty cool that's pretty  useful and also I've noticed that  sometimes when I say when I don't  actually mean to address this device it  doesn't actually I don't know how  quickly it's learning like when I'm just  talking about it it doesn't turn on but  then whenever I'm specifically  attempting to address it I must have  different voice or this is just totally  anecdotal but all on and off camera  I've been able to say the name and  either activate it or not depending on  actually what I really wanted so it's  kind of interesting when I first got it  it would activate just at anything  remotely similar to the name anyway so  yeah those are my initial thoughts I  hope that over time maybe the skill  store will evolve into something more  but I also think to an extent if you're  in it if you're a developer to yourself  which probably a lot of people on my  channel and watching this video are  there's a lot of like room for  development around this device so  Amazon's given you the platform but you  have to make something out of it and the  traditional kind of model of just make  an app slap some ads on it isn't  necessarily going to work but you have  some some real room to be pretty  creative and it makes something pretty  cool so anyways I'll leave that to you  all also on my channel I'll probably  come up with some tutorials there is a  Python API and stuff so we can work with  skills and create skills and stuff so  anyways be on the lookout for that  otherwise let me know what you guys  think of this new echo dot and basically  the entire echo line til next time

